The rule keeps the resource-first bias by balancing projected duty counts and normalized duty hours, but it normalizes by the average work time of already-opened robots instead of all possible types. It adds a centered first-maintenance balancing term that prefers robots whose next maintenance is currently projected later, and only makes low-budget robots "urgent" when `ctx.capacity` shows the current fleet is actually short of horizon coverage and maintenance can plausibly return in time. For new robots, it prefers types with high long-run duty efficiency plus active-cycle efficiency, while avoiding unnecessary overshoot when the remaining horizon is short and giving only a small tie-breaking preference to the genome's type.

